  "report_text": "\n==============================================================================\nD7 FAILURE 1 — LOOP-CONTROL FAILURE (action de-duplication)\n==============================================================================\n\nRUN 1 (before) - working agent, guard in place, normal claim\n  turns=5 tokens_in=0 tokens_out=0 cost=$0.0000 decision=approve_in_principle status=completed\n\nRUN 2 (after) - SAME agent, dedup guard REMOVED, model forced to repeat\n  turns=8 tokens_in=0 tokens_out=0 cost=$0.0000 decision=None status=stopped failure_reason=NO_FINAL_BEFORE_CAP\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. THE INSTRUMENTATION THAT FOUND IT\n   Turns and cost logged per run. RUN 2 raised no exception - it just\n   burned 8 turns (vs 5 normally) and\n   returned with failure_reason='NO_FINAL_BEFORE_CAP' instead\n   of a decision. A pass-rate table alone would show this as a failed\n   run, but nothing would tell you WHY without turn/cost instrumentation.\n\n2. THE TURN DISTRIBUTION\n   before: 5 turns | after (no guard): 8 turns | step cap: 8\n   RUN 2 hit the step cap: True\n\n3. THE FIX, AND WHY THE OTHER LAYERS WERE WRONG\n   Action de-duplication (check_duplicate_actions) is what normally\n   catches this - it fires BEFORE the repeated call is even executed.\n   The step cap did eventually stop RUN 2, but only after burning the\n   full budget with nothing to show for it - a cap BOUNDS the damage,\n   it does not DETECT the fault. A prompt-level fix cannot be relied\n   on either: the injected 'model' here is exactly the thing that\n   forgot. Only the code layer (guardrails.py) reliably remembers.\n\n4. BEFORE / AFTER, AND PROOF THE RESTORE WORKS\n   The monkey-patch in this script is undone in a finally block the\n   instant this demo ends - agent_core.py, guardrails.py, backends.py\n   are never edited on disk. The actual proof that 'putting the guard\n   back' recovers correct behaviour is your full run_eval.py result:\n   the submitted run_eval.py regression should be re-run WITH the real guard active\n   before submission. The step cap should also be checked against legitimate runs in\n   the frozen set, so restoring the\n   guard costs nothing on real cases - it only blocks the artificial\n   repeat this demo forced.\n   NOTE: a third 'guard restored + forced repeat' run is deliberately\n   NOT attempted here. This replay is a fixed, non-adaptive queue of\n   pre-recorded responses, not a real reasoning model - once the guard\n   correctly blocks the injected repeat, the replay has no sensible\n   fallback to continue with (a real model would just try something\n   else). That is a limitation of this test harness, not evidence\n   that the guard fails to recover the agent.\n==============================================================================\n"
